        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-swatch" style="background:#3B7FCC;"></div> Sales and marketing (35%)</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-swatch" style="background:var(--color-border-success);"></div> Product and R&amp;D (25%)</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-swatch" style="background:var(--color-border-warning);"></div> Operating profit (20%)</div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted-copy">Illustrative, not a universal benchmark. Mature public SaaS companies vary widely: high-growth firms reinvest nearly all margin into sales and marketing, compressing operating profit to near zero. The "Rule of 40" (revenue growth % + operating margin % ≥ 40) is the heuristic investors use to assess whether growth spend is justified.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- LTV:CAC slider -->
    <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
