        body: 'Continuing to invest in a failing project because of money, time, or reputation already spent — rather than forward-looking expected returns. Concorde is the textbook case: the UK and French governments continued funding a commercially unviable aircraft for decades because withdrawal would have meant acknowledging earlier mistakes. The rational decision at any decision point is: "given what we know now, is further investment justified by expected future returns?" Sunk costs are irrelevant. The practical difficulty is that the managers who approved the original investment are often the same managers deciding continuation — creating inescapable personal incentives to not cut losses.'
      },
