        title: 'Overconfidence Bias',
        body: 'Decision-makers systematically overestimate their ability to predict outcomes and their degree of control over events. Kahneman\'s "inside view" vs "outside view": the inside view uses detailed knowledge of the specific situation (which creates false precision); the outside view asks "what happens to projects like this?" (which is sobering). M&A research shows that 70–90% of acquisitions fail to create value for the acquirer — yet every board approves acquisitions confident they will manage the integration better than the historical average. Pre-mortem exercises (imagining the project has failed and working backward) reduce overconfidence by forcing decision-makers to engage with failure scenarios.'
