        body: 'The tendency for the first number seen to disproportionately influence subsequent estimates, even when irrelevant. In salary negotiations, the first number offered sets the anchor — studies show that random high or low numbers given to experimental subjects before negotiation exercises materially shift final outcomes. In M&A, the initial valuation range anchors subsequent negotiation. The practical implication: whoever sets the first number in a financial negotiation gains a structural advantage. Awareness of anchoring does not fully eliminate it — the effect persists even when subjects are explicitly told the anchor number is random.'
