        body: 'Irving Janis\'s 1972 concept describes the deterioration of decision-making quality when a cohesive group prioritises harmony and conformity over realistic appraisal. Symptoms: illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, belief in the group\'s moral superiority, pressure on dissenters, and self-censorship. The Bay of Pigs invasion (1961) is the canonical case: Kennedy\'s cabinet suppressed doubts about an obviously flawed plan because everyone assumed someone smarter had vetted it. The structural fix is designated Devil\'s Advocate roles — but research shows that explicitly appointed Devil\'s Advocates are often less effective than genuine disagreers because the group discounts their position as performative.'
      },
